\section{Simulation Stress Testing Protocol for Project Axon--FP}
\author{C.~Matthew~Blackwell}

\subsection{Overview}
This section documents the full stress-testing protocol applied to the Axon--FP real-time astrophysical simulation engine. The objective is to evaluate system stability, numerical convergence, and hardware saturation behavior under maximal synthetic load conditions. All subsystems---astrometric ingestion, gravitational field computation, habitability modeling, and civilizational percolation PDEs---were simultaneously driven to peak operational limits.

\subsection{Stress Testing Parameters}

\subsubsection{Astrometric Ingestion Load}
To evaluate upper-bound throughput, the ingestion pipeline was scaled from the standard
Gaia DR3 + APOGEE-2 dataset (1.5 billion sources) to a synthetic multi-epoch dataset of
\[
10.2 \times 10^9 \text{ sources}.
\]
Spatial indexing utilized HEALPix tessellation combined with dynamic octree partitioning.
Tree-depth rebalancing latency remained below
\[
2.0\text{ ms},
\]
with an observed peak of \(1.4\text{ ms}\), and zero coordinate loss.

\subsubsection{N-Body Gravitational and Trajectory Load}
The galactic potential model \(\Phi_{MW}\) was computed across
\[
1.2 \times 10^9
\]
stellar bodies. Parallel execution was distributed across a Rust/Rayon thread pool of 1,024 workers, with GPU acceleration via OpenCL/CUDA. Peak utilization reached
\[
99.2\%
\]
with no thermal-induced divergence.

\subsubsection{Percolation Kernel Saturation}
The civilizational density PDE,
\[
\frac{\partial \rho_{civ}}{\partial t}
= S - \nabla\cdot(v_{ext}\rho_{civ}) - \gamma_{extinct}\rho_{civ},
\]
was executed under maximal wavefront overlap conditions. Dampening was set to zero to test stability under high-gradient propagation. Real-time visualization maintained
\[
60\text{ FPS}
\]
throughout.

